; AccountHold.addon
;
; ESO Add-On manifest. Renamed from .txt to .addon per console amendment
; A2.1 — `.addon` is forward-compatible on PC and required on console after
; 2025-06-04. All file references below MUST match on-disk casing exactly
; (PS5 is case-sensitive; PC and Xbox are tolerant).
;
; APIVersion verified from https://wiki.esoui.com/APIVersion (2026-05-15):
;   Live: 101049    PTS: 101050
; Update both values when ESO ships a new patch.
;
; VERSIONING (Microsoft date-based scheme): Major.Minor.YYMMDD.Revision
;   * Major/Minor    — bumped for feature / breaking changes. Major stays 0
;                      while the addon is PRE-RELEASE / in testing; bump to 1
;                      for the first GA (general availability) release.
;   * YYMMDD         — the build date (two-digit year, month, day).
;   * Revision       — 0-based counter for multiple builds on the same day.
; The `## Version` string carries the full four-part value for humans; the
; `## AddOnVersion` integer (required to be a monotonically increasing int by
; ESO's dependency system) encodes the same build as YYMMDD * 100 + Revision.
;
; WORKED EXAMPLE — a build made on 3 Nov 2025, second build of that day.
; (Illustrative values only; the real directives are the uncommented `##`
; lines below. Every line here starts with `;` so ESO ignores it.)
;     Version:      0.1.251103.1      <- EXACTLY four dot-separated parts
;     AddOnVersion: 25110301          <- 251103 * 100 + 1
; Write the date as ONE six-digit YYMMDD field. Do NOT expand it to a
; four-digit year or split it with dots ("0.1.2025.11.03.1" is six parts and
; does not match this scheme — earlier builds drifted into that form).
; Bump `## Version` and AccountHold.lua's `AccountHold.version` together;
; they must always be identical strings.

## Title: Account Hold
## Author: AccountHold contributors
## Version: 0.1.261017.0
## AddOnVersion: 26101700
## APIVersion: 101049 101050
## SavedVariables: AccountHoldSV
## Description: Account-wide inventory index with per-item and per-set holds. Auto-routes reserved items between characters via any shared container.

; Optional dependencies:
;   * LibHarvensAddonSettings (LHAS) — used by ui/Settings.lua to render the
;     settings panel. Works on PC and console.
;
;     NOT bundled. lib/ currently contains only a README. The addon does not
;     declare DependsOn / OptionalDependsOn either; instead
;     Platform.GetSettingsBackend() looks up the GLOBAL `LibHarvensAddonSettings`
;     at call time, so it is satisfied by ANY source — most commonly LHAS
;     installed as its own standalone add-on (it is available on console via the
;     Bethesda.net add-on catalogue, which is why the panel works on Xbox).
;
;     If nothing provides that global the add-on still loads normally; only the
;     settings panel is suppressed. See lib/README.md before attempting to embed
;     a copy — embedding also requires adding the library's .lua files to the
;     file list below, because ESO does not auto-load nested add-on folders.

# Strings first so SI_* identifiers exist before any module reads them.
localization/en.lua

# Namespace, lifecycle, EVENT_ADD_ON_LOADED gate, SavedVariables wiring.
AccountHold.lua

# Foundation layer (src/core). Dependency-free building blocks that everything
# below MAY use and nothing below is required to use -- existing modules keep
# their own hand-rolled helpers until each is migrated deliberately, with its
# own test. Loaded here, immediately after the namespace exists, so no later
# file can be ordered before them.
#
# Order within the layer is significant: Log installs itself as Safe's failure
# reporter at load time, so Safe must already exist.
src/core/Safe.lua
src/core/Text.lua
src/core/Log.lua
# Surface: the declarative install / teardown / gating contract. Loaded after
# Log so it can report through it. Nothing is required to register with it --
# adoption is per-surface and incremental.
src/core/Surface.lua

# Maintainer-edited access data (whole-add-on + per-feature allowlists). Plain
# Lua (no parser dependency); loaded before src/Features.lua reads it.
config/FeatureAccess.lua

# Core modules. Order is significant: each reads earlier ones.
src/Platform.lua
# Per-feature access gates (Epic 0001). After config/FeatureAccess.lua and
# before any feature module that calls Features:IsEnabled.
src/Features.lua
src/Index.lua
src/Holds.lua
src/Notify.lua
src/Mover.lua
src/Scanner.lua
src/Input.lua

# Epic 0008 (QoL) model layer. Counts and clears "new item" markers across every
# surface that carries one. Owns no UI, so the same model can back a keybind, a
# settings action or a slash command. After src/core (it uses Safe) and before
# any UI that surfaces it.
src/Notifications.lua

# Live travel tracing to chat (BUGS.md QMQ-1). Wraps the unprotected
# FastTravelToNode so every jump the player makes -- INCLUDING manual ones from
# the world map -- prints the node it used. That is the only way to learn what a
# dungeon's travel node is actually called. Off by default; toggled in settings.
src/TravelTrace.lua

# Epic model modules. After src/Index.lua (they query it) and before the UI
# that renders them. Each is inert until its feature gate is enabled.
#
# Bundled set-acquisition data first, then its accessor, then the two consumers:
# ESO exposes no set->activity mapping (GetItemSetCollectionPieceInfo returns
# only pieceId + slot), so this hand-maintained table is the only source of it.
data/setSources.lua
src/SetSources.lua
src/BuildCreator.lua
# Equip / auto-equip / Armory-export engine for those builds (reads BuildCreator).
src/BuildEquip.lua
src/Priorities.lua
src/Travel.lua
src/DungeonQueue.lua

# UI: XML virtual templates first, then Lua scene wiring.
ui/AccountHold.xml
ui/InventoryTab_Gamepad.lua
ui/InventoryTab_Keyboard.lua
ui/HoldDialog.lua
ui/BankActionPanel.lua
ui/BankTab_Gamepad.lua
# Epic 0005 UI. Screen before menu: the menu install fails closed when its
# target screen is missing, and its entry shares ZO_MENU_ENTRIES with
# OPTIONS/QUIT/LOG_OUT, so a dangling entry there is not survivable.
ui/PrioritiesScreen_Gamepad.lua
ui/PrioritiesMenu_Gamepad.lua
ui/PrioritiesSetsBook_Gamepad.lua
ui/ArmoryScreen_Gamepad.lua
ui/DungeonFinder_Gamepad.lua
ui/DungeonFinderScene_Gamepad.lua
# Epic 0008 QoL: hold-to-clear on the gamepad Collections book. Negotiates its
# keybind slot at runtime rather than claiming one -- Y is already taken in
# three of that screen's four keybind groups. See
# docs/research/API_REFERENCE.md, "Gamepad collections book".
ui/CollectionsClearAll_Gamepad.lua
ui/Settings.lua

# (No Bindings.xml — on PC the addon is driven from the third
# "Account Gear" inventory tab; on console/gamepad the entry point is an
# "Quartermaster" button attached to the Character screen keybind strip.
# No PC-only keybind required.)
